course: dictionary
lesson: 6660
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around the word, and ask: what is this sentence about? «Cloves and nutmeg grew only on a few tiny islands in the Moluccas, in today's Indonesia.» The words grew, islands and Indonesia tell you that nutmeg is a plant product, so it is the spice.
explain: Nutmeg can also be a verb, an action word. «He nutmegged the defender and ran past him.» Here nutmegged is something a player does, so it is a verb. Football players say a nutmeg for a trick where the ball goes through someone's legs.
explain: To check, put your meaning into the sentence. If you say «He kicked the ball between the defender's legs and ran past him», it still makes sense, so nutmeg is the trick. If you put «a spice» in the football sentence, it does not make sense, so that meaning is wrong.
fact: Nutmeg is a brown spice made by grating the hard seed of a tropical tree.
fact: Nutmeg is used in cakes, custard and drinks, such as eggnog.
fact: Long ago, nutmeg was very valuable, and people fought over the tiny islands where it grew.
fact: In football slang, a nutmeg is a cheeky trick: you kick the ball between someone's legs and run past them.
fact: When the context gives a clear clue, you can work out which meaning of a word is being used.
exercise: «Cloves and nutmeg grew only on a few tiny islands in the Moluccas.» Where did nutmeg grow? | Everywhere in Europe | In deserts | On the moon | *On a few tiny islands
exercise: In playground football, what is it called when the ball goes between someone's legs and you run past them? | A corner | A header | A penalty | *A nutmeg
exercise: Which of these would you most likely flavour with nutmeg? | Concrete | Sand | *Rice pudding | Football boots
test: «Pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg were very valuable in medieval Europe.» Which word tells you they were worth a lot? | medieval | passed | tropical | *valuable
test: «He nutmegged the defender and ran past him.» What kind of word is nutmegged here? | A noun, a thing | *A verb, an action | A place | An adjective, a describing word
exam: What is nutmeg made from? | *The hard seed of a tropical tree | Tree bark | Stones from the sea | Leaves of a palm
